From: EspenJD <106583432+EspenJD@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:54:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(nvidia): read every GDDR7 chip on clamshell cards

A GDDR7 clamshell card has four valid temperature words per frame buffer
partition: IC0_SUBP0, IC0_SUBP1, IC1_SUBP0 and IC1_SUBP1. Each word is one
chip. The code read only the first valid word of a subpartition (IC0) and
showed its bits 23:16 as "(Front)" and its bits 31:24 as "(Back)". On GDDR7
all four bytes of a word hold the same code, so that was one chip shown
twice, and the IC1 chips were never read.

Raw register dumps of an RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition and a
Max-Q (32 chips each, 600 samples) show 32 valid words per card. The IC1
words differ from the IC0 words by 2 to 4 degC at idle and change on their
own.

On a GDDR7 clamshell card, read every valid word and name the sensors
"A0 (IC0)" and "A0 (IC1)". The board side of IC0 and IC1 is not known, so
the names do not say Front or Back. GDDR6X and single-sided GDDR7 cards
keep their sensors and names.

The register logic moves to its own module with the register read as a
closure, so that it has unit tests. Two of them use the raw words of the
two cards.
